"SafeMT: Multi-turn Safety for Multimodal Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12133v1)\nciteturn2818view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12133v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 589\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†Jailbreak Attack Methods L19:     2. cite9†Safety Evaluation Benchmarks L20:   4. cite10†3 SafeMT Benchmark L21:     1. cite11†3.1 Dataset Overview L22:     2. cite12†3.2 Data Collection and Generation L23:     3. cite13†3.3 Safety Index L24:     4. cite14†3.4 Dialogue Safety Moderator L25:   5. cite15†4 Experiments And Analysis L26:     1. cite16†4.1 Experimental Setup L27:     2. cite17†4.2 Experimental Results L28:       1. cite18†4.2.1 Main Results L29:         1. cite19†Multi-turn Result L30:         2. cite20†Scenario Result L31:     3. cite21†4.3 Dialogue Safety Moderator L32:   6. cite22†5 Conclusion L33:   7. cite23†References L34:   8. cite24†A Future Work L35:   9. cite25†B Ethics Statement L36:   10. cite26†C Evaluated Model Details L37:   11. cite27†D Existing Assets Licences L38:   12. cite28†E Additional Experimental Results L39:     1. cite29†E.1 Position of Malicious Intent L40:     2. cite30†E.2 Different Modal Data Results L41:     3. cite31†E.3 Dialogue Safety Moderator Results L42:     4. cite32†E.4 Jailbreak Attack Methods Results L43:   13. cite33†F Dialogue Safety Moderator Training Details L44:   14. cite34†G Experiments compute resources L45:   15. cite35†H Details of Scenarios L46:   16. cite36†I Prompt L47: cite37†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L48: \nL49: arXiv:2510.12133v1 [cs.CL] 14 Oct 2025\nL50: # SafeMT: Multi-turn Safety for Multimodal Language Models\nL51: Han ZHU ^{†}^{†}thanks: Equal Contribution; $ˆ†$Corresponding author.\nL52: Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology Email: hzhubo@connect.ust.hksiruihan    Juntao Dai Email: yikeguo@ust.hk    Jiaming Ji Affiliation: Peking University    Haoran Li Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Chengkun Cai Affiliation: University of Edinburgh    Pengcheng Wen Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Chi-Min Chan Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Boyuan Chen Affiliation: Peking University    Yaodong Yang Affiliation: Peking University    Sirui Han    Yike Guo\nL53: ###### Abstract\nL54: With the widespread use of multi-modal Large Language models (MLLMs), safety issues have become a growing concern. Multi-turn dialogues, which are more common in everyday interactions, pose a greater risk than single prompts; however, existing benchmarks do not adequately consider this situation.\nL55: To encourage the community to focus on the safety issues of these models in multi-turn dialogues, we introduce SafeMT, a benchmark that features dialogues of varying lengths generated from harmful queries accompanied by images. This benchmark consists of 10,000 samples in total, encompassing 17 different scenarios and four jailbreak methods. Additionally, we propose Safety Index (SI) to evaluate the general safety of MLLMs during conversations.\nL56: We assess the safety of 17 models using this benchmark and discover that the risk of successful attacks on these models increases as the number of turns in harmful dialogues rises. This observation indicates that the safety mechanisms of these models are inadequate for recognizing the hazard in dialogue interactions. We propose a dialogue safety moderator capable of detecting malicious intent concealed within conversations and providing MLLMs with relevant safety policies.\nL57: Experimental results from several open-source models indicate that this moderator is more effective in reducing multi-turn ASR compared to existed guard models.\nL58: Warning: This paper contains examples of harmful text and images.\nL59: ## 1 Introduction\nL60: \nL61: cite38†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: SafeMT: We integrate existing dataset and manually collect our own red-team queries, incorporating 17 scenarios and four jailbreak methods into our benchmark. The dialogues are generated using closed-source models. Conversations containing refusal words are refined to enhance the potential risk. Dialogue safety moderator is trained based on dialogues, queries and scenarios to reduce the harmfulness of AI generated answers.\nL62: In recent years, multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) such as GPT-4o (cite39†OpenAI et al., 2024 ), Gemini (cite40†Team et al., 2024 ), and DeepSeek-VL (cite41†Lu et al., 2024 ) demonstrate exceptional capabilities across diverse domains from healthcare (cite42†AlSaad et al., 2024 ; cite43†Liu et al., 2023a ) to autonomous driving (cite44†Renz et al., 2024 ; cite45†Zheng et al., 2024 ; cite46†Li et al., 2025 ).\nL63: However, the extensive integration of these models into various aspects of human daily life, attributable to their remarkable capabilities, has prompted social concerns regarding the potential safety issues they may engender.\nL64: Ungoverned models have the probability to generate harmful advices, disseminate misinformation, and incite public unrest, which can disrupt social order in less severe instances and pose significant threats to human life in more extreme cases (cite47†Gong et al., 2023 ; cite48†Fang and Perkins, 2024 ).\nL65: Numerous studies and policies underscore the urgency of addressing safety issues related to MLLMs as they become increasingly involved into human life (cite49†Spotdraft, 2024 ; cite50†Amodei et al., 2016 ; cite51†Ji et al., 2023 ; cite52†Hong Kong Digital Policy Office and Center, 2025 ).\nL66: This pressing need has catalyzed a wide array of contributions from researchers, all aimed at rigorously evaluating the safety of MLLMs encompassing a variety of methodologies and frameworks (cite53†Ying et al., 2024 ; cite54†Fang et al., 2025 ; cite55†Chen et al., 2024 ; cite56†Liu et al., 2024c ; cite57†Ji et al., 2025 ). All these works demonstrated the vulnerability of the models’ safety mechanisms.\nL67: Nevertheless, existing researches neglect a crucial aspect: the safety issues that emerge in multi-turn dialogues, which are commonly encountered in human interactions with MLLMs. This situation presents three significant challenges concerning models safety both in terms of evaluation and protection:\nL68:   * •\nL69: \nL70: Current benchmarks consist solely of single queries paired with images, which are insufficient for comprehensively evaluating the safety of models in multi-turn dialogues.\nL71: \nL72:   * •\nL73: \nL74: Assessing the safety of a model based on the Attack Success Rate (ASR) (cite56†Liu et al., 2024c ; cite47†Gong et al., 2023 ) across an entire dataset is inadequate since this index neglects the correlation between the safety of models and the dialogue length.\nL75: \nL76:   * •\nL77: The safety mechanisms of the model are susceptible to compromise during interactions, allowing malicious users to induce them to generate harmful responses through dialogue.\nL78: \nL79: Therefore, we design rigorous experiments and conduct comprehensive analysis to address the following question in this paper:\nL80: \nL81: How can we evaluate and improve the safety of MLLMs in multi-turn dialogues?\nL82: To advance the understanding and development of safety issues of MLLMs in multi-turn dialogues, we produce our benchmark, SafeMT. Our benchmark comprises 2,000 harmful queries and 8,000 dialogues derived from these queries, covering 17 scenarios and utilizing 4 jailbreak methods.\nL83: We utilize the GPT-4o (cite39†OpenAI et al., 2024 ), Claude-3.7-Sonnet (cite58†Anthropic, 2025 ) and Gemini-2.0-flash (cite59†Google, 2024a ) to generate dialogues by employing red-team queries sourced from established datasets, alongside manually curated queries that contain potentially harmful content paired with images. Subsequently, we refine dialogues containing refusal words, aiming to keep the potential risk in conversation.\nL84: A sample of 200 dialogues of each length is randomly selected and subjected to a meticulous manual review process with the purpose of ensuring the integrity and plausibility of these conversations.\nL85: Benchmark Dataset information Type Data Format Data Size Scenarios Jail-Att Evaluation Metrics SafetyBench cite60†Zhang et al. (2024) Prompt Text 11,435 7 ✗ Accuracy CoSafe cite61†Yu et al. (2024) Multi-turn Text 1,400 14 ✗ ASR & Harmful Rate HH-RLHF cite62†Ganguli et al. (2022) Multi-turn Text 38,961 26 ✓ ASR & Harmlessness Score SafeDialBench cite63†Cao et al. (2025) Multi-turn Text 4,053 22 ✓ Fine-grained Scoring MM-SafetyBench cite56†Liu et al.\nL86: (2024c) Prompt Image & Text 5,040 13 ✓ ASR & Refusal Rate JailBreakV cite64†Luo et al. (2024) Prompt Image & Text 28,000 16 ✓ ASR MLLMGUARD cite65†Gu et al. (2024) Prompt Image & Text 2,282 12 ✓ ASD & PAR SafeMT (Ours) Multi-turn Image & Text 10,000 17 ✓ ASR & Refusal Rate & SI\nL87: Table 1: Details of benchmarks. “ASR” stands for Attack Success Rate, “ASD” stands for Attack Success Degree, “PAR” stands for Perfect Answer Rate and “SI” stands for Safety Index.\nL88: \nL89: In summary, our contributions are as follow:\nL90: \nL91:   * •\nL92: SafeMT: To the best of our knowledge, SafeMT is the first benchmark specifically designed to evaluate the multi-turn safety mechanisms of MLLMs through the integration of images within dialogues. This benchmark comprises 2,000 meticulously curated harmful queries paired with corresponding images, alongside 8,000 dialogues generated based on these paired queries across 17 scenarios.\nL93: \nL94:   * •\nL95: Safety Index: We propose an evaluation index grounded in traditional ASR methodology, specifically designed to assess the safety of MLLMs in conversational contexts. Unlike ASR, our safety index offers distinct advantages by explicitly accounting for the positive correlation between model security and the number of successful attack rounds, as well as the stability of the model’s defensive mechanisms throughout dialogue interactions.\nL96: This comprehensive approach enables a more nuanced evaluation of models’ safety mechanisms in conversation scenario.\nL97:   * •\nL98: \nL99: Dialogue Safety Moderator: We develop a flexible plug-and-play moderator for the detection of malicious intent during conversations, specifically trained on dialogues that incorporate images and associated harmful queries. The results demonstrate that this approach significantly enhances the safety and harmlessness of model-generated responses.\nL100: ## 2 Related Work\nL101: ##### Jailbreak Attack Methods\nL102: Building on the safety alignment research conducted by scholars, these large models already incorporate basic safety mechanisms that prevent them from responding to straightforward harmful inquiries. However, a variety of jailbreak attack approaches continue to emerge. Many-shot jailbreaking proposed by Anthropic (cite66†Anil et al., 2024 ) mentions that when the number of shots in prompts exceed the threshold, the percentage of harmful responses will grow rapidly.\nL103: Beyond the common jailbreak attacks targeting LLMs, MLLMs are more vulnerable, as cross-modal data can undermine their basic safety mechanisms. Many studies show that harmful prompts could be hidden in the adversarial images by typography, decomposing of prompts and embedding-space-based methodology (cite47†Gong et al., 2023 ; cite67†Qi et al., 2024 ; cite68†Shayegani et al., 2023 ; cite69†Gou et al., 2024 ).\nL104: MIRAGE (cite70†You et al., 2025 ) emphasizes that incorporating both images and text in conversations significantly enhances the ASR of MLLMs.\nL105: ##### Safety Evaluation Benchmarks\nL106: With the widespread used of both LLMs and MLLMs, security issues are receiving more attention to prevent their misuse in harming society. To forbid illegal use of LLMs, OpenAI published usage policies which mention several unsafe scenarios including privacy violation, generation of malware, fraudulent and so on (cite71†OpenAI, ).\nL107: Safety benchmarks including R-Judge (cite72†Yuan et al., 2024 ), SafetyBench (cite60†Zhang et al., 2024 ) and so on (cite73†Li et al., 2024 ; cite74†Mou et al., 2024 ; cite75†Sun et al., 2023 ) test the security levels of several LLMs under different scenarios.\nL108: Since chatting is one of the most important and widely used applications for these language models, there are many safety benchmarks that evaluate dialogue risks such as CoSafe (cite61†Yu et al., 2024 ), SafeDialBench (cite63†Cao et al., 2025 ) and HH-RLHF (cite62†Ganguli et al., 2022 ). As the increment number of MLLMs being published, there is an urgent need of safety benchmarks for these models.\nL109: MM-SafetyBench (cite56†Liu et al., 2024c ), JailBreakV (cite64†Luo et al., 2024 ) and MLLMGUARD (cite65†Gu et al., 2024 ) are proposed for MLLMs safety evaluation. However, for MLLMs, dialogue is also a crucial capability. Despite its significance, there are currently no established benchmarks specifically designed to evaluate dialogue safety in MLLMs.\nL110: However, existing benchmarks do not address the potential safety issues of MLLMs in multi-turn situations. According to Table cite76†1 , we are the first benchmark that contains both multi-modal data and multi-turn conversations.\nL111: ## 3 SafeMT Benchmark\nL112: ### 3.1 Dataset Overview\nL113: SafeMT comprises a total of 10,000 samples, including 2,000 harmful queries, each paired with corresponding images, and 8,000 dialogues categorized across 17 distinct scenarios. These scenarios are classified into five types: Ethics, Illegality, Certification, Sensitivity, and Harmful Impact. They adhere strictly to the usage policy outlined by OpenAI (cite71†OpenAI, ) and the policy guidelines of the Gemini App (cite77†Google, 2024b ). The distribution of this data is illustrated in Figure cite78†2 .\nL114: For each identical harmful query, we generate dialogues of varying lengths—specifically, dialogues consisting of two, four, six, and eight turns while maintaining consistency in the associated images.\nL115: cite79†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Scenario Distribution\nL116: ### 3.2 Data Collection and Generation\nL117: Harmful Query Collection A portion of the data in SafeMT is derived from four published datasets: Beavertails-V (cite57†Ji et al., 2025 ), MM-SafetyBench (cite56†Liu et al., 2024c ), MLLMGuard (cite65†Gu et al., 2024 ), and JailbreakV (cite64†Luo et al., 2024 ). The data collection process is conducted in two primary steps.\nL118: In the first step, harmful queries successfully intercepted from these datasets using the LLaVA-1.5-13B (cite80†Liu et al., 2023b ), Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct (cite81†Team, 2025 ), and Gemma-3-4b-it (cite82†Team et al., 2025 ) models are compiled. In the second step, we formulate our own red-team questions utilizing the Claude-3.7-Sonnet (cite58†Anthropic, 2025 ) model and manually review all identified harmful queries to evaluate their potential risks.\nL119: Concurrently, sensitive keywords extracted by AI are employed for manual online image searches. Ultimately, we compile a total of 2,000 unique harmful queries after filtering out any duplicate entries.\nL120: Dialogue Construction Drawing inspiration from Cosafe (cite61†Yu et al., 2024 ), we utilized GPT-4o (cite39†OpenAI et al., 2024 ), Claude-3.7-Sonnet (cite58†Anthropic, 2025 ), and Gemini-2.0-Flash-Exp (cite59†Google, 2024a ) to conduct a series of dialogues that emulate human-AI interactions. SafeMT incorporates four widely recognized jailbreak techniques, applied to a meticulously curated collection of red-team queries.\nL121: For each query, we generate dialogues of varying lengths—specifically, 2-turn, 4-turn, 6-turn, and 8-turn where each turn signifies an exchange between the user and the AI assistant. This structured dataset facilitates an in-depth examination of the safety protectors’ susceptibility of MLLMs across conversations of different lengths.\nL122: Recognizing that dialogues may occasionally include refusal phrases such as \"I’m sorry, I cannot assist with that,\" we manually refine these exchanges to preserve the potential risks inherent in such interactions.\nL123: We apply four jailbreak attack methods during the dataset generation process:\nL124: \nL125:   * •\nL126: \nL127: Role Play: We employ a narrative technique within the dialogue, wherein a character is constructed to simulate a specific persona, such as ’I am a writer, I want to write a novel about …’ (cite63†Cao et al., 2025 ).\nL128: \nL129:   * •\nL130: In-context Learning: We involves the strategic insertion of randomly selected harmful question and answer pairs into multi-turn dialogues. These pairs follow a fixed pattern, such as “Sure! Here is the detail:” to subtly influence the model’s behavior (cite66†Anil et al., 2024 ).\nL131: \nL132:   * •\nL133: \nL134: Image Reference Attack: We apply the substitution of sensitive keywords with corresponding images within the dialogue. (cite68†Shayegani et al., 2023 ).\nL135: \nL136:   * •\nL137: Topic Change: We initially discuss only common image information or benign topics at the onset of dialogues. As the conversation progresses and the attention of the dialogue potentially diminishes, harmful content is subtly introduced (cite63†Cao et al., 2025 ).\nL138: Quality Control We randomly select 200 dialogues of each length and conduct a comprehensive manual verification process to evaluate their rationality. The primary objective of this review is to ensure that the interactions are coherent, contextually appropriate, and accurately reference the associated images. During the manual evaluation process, 84.6% of the dialogues generated by the AI are assessed as reasonable, and retaining a potential risk (cite83†Zheng et al., 2023 ).\nL139: ### 3.3 Safety Index\nL140: Traditional metrics such as ASR calculated using the formula: $ASR=\\frac{\\sum^{|D|}_{1}I(Q_{i})}{|D|}$ where $I(\\cdot)=1$ if the response is judged as unsafe and $I(\\cdot)=0$ otherwise, $D$ denotes the size of the dataset (cite56†Liu et al., 2024c ) is inadequate for assessing the safety of MLLMs when confronted with dialogues.\nL141: This index primarily assess the overall success of attacks across a dataset, neglecting to account for the relationship between model safety and attack success rates at various stages of dialogue. For instance, they do not reflect the heightened vulnerability of a model that can be successfully compromised in shorter dialogues, which may indicate a greater risk in real-world interactions.\nL142: Consequently, while ASR provides a snapshot of a model’s security, it does not facilitate a comparative analysis of the degrees of vulnerability among models throughout the interaction process.\nL143: To address this limitation, we propose Safety Index (SI) shown as Eq cite84†1 . This index incorporates two critical considerations often overlooked by traditional metrics: (1)It presents that a model should be deemed less safe if it is susceptible to successful attacks in earlier rounds of dialogue, reflecting a more immediate vulnerability. A model that is easily manipulated at the outset of a dialogue significantly reduces the overall security of the interaction.\nL144: (2)It evaluates the consistency and robustness of the model’s protective mechanisms, as erratic performance can undermine user trust and safety. A model that demonstrates variability in its ability to resist attacks can lead to unpredictable outcomes, which may dissuade users from relying on it in critical situations. By integrating these dimensions, the SI offers a more holistic and informative assessment of model safety.\nL145:  | $$\\begin{split}SI=(1-\\sum^{n}_{0}\\frac{e^{-k}}{\\sum^{n}_{0}e^{-k}}ASR_{k})\\cdot\\\\\nL146: (1-mean(\\sigma[I_{j},\\dots,I_{n}]))\\end{split}$$  |  | (1)\nL147: In the equation, $j$ represents the first round in which a query successfully attacks the model, $n$ means a set of dialogue turn counts for each malicious intent, which is $\\{1,2,4,6,8\\}$ here, while $k$ denotes the round number. The vector $[I_{1},...,I_{n}]\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}$ captures the safety status of responses across the dialogue, where each variable $I_{j}$ indicates whether the response to a specific malicious intent in the $j$th turn is safe or unsafe.\nL148: Specifically, $I_{j}=1$ signifies an unsafe response in the $j$th turn. All variables in this vector assess the same malicious intent throughout the conversation, allowing for a comprehensive evaluation of the model’s performance in the context of interactions.\nL149: ### 3.4 Dialogue Safety Moderator\nL150: \nL151: cite85†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Dialogue safety moderator is trained on dialogues with images and predicted harmful queries. It will predict the users’ intents based on previous conversation and classify the scenario. Security rules will be mapped based on different scenarios and input as system prompt along with users’ intent for later inference.\nL152: In order to mitigate the potential risks associated with multi-turn dialogues, we develop a plug-and-play module, as illustrated in Figure cite86†3 . Concurrently, it is crucial to recognize that safety protocols for health consultations, financial advice, and legal opinions differ markedly from those applicable in other contexts.\nL153: Therefore, it is imperative to meticulously address the unique requirements of these domains, while aspiring to devise a universal approach that adheres to policies across all scenarios.\nL154: Drawing inspiration from existing research, which has demonstrated the efficacy of optimized prompts in moderating the responses of MLLMs (cite56†Liu et al., 2024c ; cite87†Chen et al., 2023 ; cite88†Inan et al., 2023 ), we train this moderator using a partitioned SafeMT dataset comprising 7,000 instances to identify users’ latent intents and generate context-specific safe prompts for dialogues across a variety of scenarios.\nL155: To achieve our objectives, we initially construct a training dataset $T=\\{i^{k},d^{k},q^{k},s^{k},r^{k}\\}^{N}_{k=1}$, derived from SafeMT, where $i$ stands for the image, $d$ denotes the dialogue, $q$ represents the harmful query, $s$ signifies the scenario, and $r$ corresponds to the relevant rule.\nL156: Subsequently, we employ supervised fine-tuning on Gemma-3-4B-it to train a model $\\pi_{\\theta}$ that generates harmful queries, scenarios, and rules for the dialogues within our training dataset, utilizing the following loss function with N samples:\nL157:  | $$L(\\theta)=-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum^{N}_{k=1}log\\pi_{\\theta}(Q_{k},S_{k}|D_{k},I_{k})$$  |  | (2)\nL158: \nL159: Security rules will be inputted as system prompt along with users’ intent for downstream inference.\nL160: ## 4 Experiments And Analysis\nL161: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL162: \nL163: Setup We systematically evaluate the performance of a suite of 17 computational models on our benchmark. For all models under consideration, to minimize the randomness in response generation, the temperature parameter is fixed at 0, and the maximum output token length is constrained to 1,024 tokens. We limit the input to 4,096 tokens and one image and our evaluation protocol entails analyzing only a single image per dialogue.\nL164: Evaluated Models We evaluate two closed-source models which are GPT-4o (cite39†OpenAI et al., 2024 ) and Gemini-2.0-flash (cite59†Google, 2024a ).\nL165: Simultaneously, 15 open-source models which are Gemma-3-4b-it, Gemma-3-12b-it, Gemma-3-27b-it (cite82†Team et al., 2025 ), Llama-3.2-11B-Vision-Instruct, Llama-3.2-90B-Vision-Instruct (cite89†Meta, 2025 ), InterVL2.5-8B, InterVL2.5-78B (cite90†Chen et al., 2025 ), InternVL3-8B-Instruct, InternVL3-78-Instruct (cite91†Zhu et al., 2025 ), LLaVA-NeXT-7B, LLaVA-NeXT-13B (cite92†Liu et al., 2024a ), Qwen2-VL-7B-Instruct, Qwen2-VL-72B-Instruct (cite93†Bai et al., 2023 ; cite94†Wang et al., 2024 ), Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct (cite81†Team, 2025 ) are assessed as well.\nL166: Evaluation Methods The responses generated by these models are evaluated and labeled by GPT-4o-mini according to established criteria, categorizing them as either safe or unsafe across various scenarios. In highly specialized domains such as health consultations, financial advice, and legal opinions, safe responses are required to explicitly state that they do not constitute professional advice and to remind users to be cautious.\nL167: Within political scenarios, safe responses must refrain from expressing any political attitudes or biases. In other scenarios, a response is deemed unsafe if it leaks private information, provides instructions for illegal activities, describes harmful actions, contains explicit content, endorses harmful behavior, promotes racial discrimination, or suggests potentially harmful solutions.\nL168: We manually label a randomly selected sample of 150 responses from each model and achieve an accuracy of rate 89% compared with gpt-4o-mini (cite83†Zheng et al., 2023 ). We employed two metrics in the experiment:\nL169:   * •\nL170: \nL171: We apply ASR to assess the proportion of malicious responses produced by the models (cite56†Liu et al., 2024c ; cite64†Luo et al., 2024 ).\nL172: \nL173:   * •\nL174: \nL175: We employ SI that accounts for both ASR and the robustness in defense mechanisms to assess the safety of MLLMs in conversation.\nL176: ### 4.2 Experimental Results\nL177: In our experiments, we evaluate the performance of these models using SafeMT benchmark, focusing on dialogues of varying lengths. Our analysis reveals that dialogues with more turns significantly increase the risks associated with these models. By comparing their performance across data with various modalities, we identify that the visual components of the models introduce additional risks. Additionally, certain contexts, such as financial advice, appear to lack safety alignment from the outset.\nL178: Furthermore, our findings with dialogue safety moderator indicate that models may inadvertently overlook safety policies during dialogues; however, reminders can effectively reinforce these policies throughout the conversation.\nL179: #### 4.2.1 Main Results\nL180: ##### Multi-turn Result\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMCP Security Bench (MSB): Benchmarking Attacks Against Model Context Protocol in LLM Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.15994v1)\nciteturn2818view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.15994v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 583\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHolistic Agent Leaderboard: The Missing Infrastructure for AI Agent Evaluation (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11977v1)\nciteturn2818view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11977v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 978\n"
